# ترجمة 12–16
T = {
"11": {
"en": """I am not old enough for stories to flow from my face like water from streams, nor am I a boy whose face has never been touched by the pens of fate, the claws of waiting, and the mud of experience. I am in the middle of my life. I stand now at the peak of life’s pyramid, having once looked up at it as the end of the road while I climbed through the years, full of pride. Now I look out over a frightening slope and a cold gray expanse, and at the bottom of the slope lies the finish line. All I hope for is to descend in peace to that last foothill, so that I may bid the journey farewell in peace. I have not married yet, and I don’t think I ever will, for life is shorter than the wedding ceremonies themselves. I graduated from the faculty of science, in microbiology. In truth, that has helped me deal with many of the germs I meet every day: my manager, the rude customers, and the passersby, “the creatures of Doctor Moreau” from H. G. Wells’s novel. I work as a supervisor of laborers at a contracting company, and my degree, as you notice, has nothing to do with defining this job. In my youth I read the greatest works of literature. I read Shakespeare, Goethe, Jane Austen, Edgar Allan Poe, Hans Andersen, Dickens, Mark Twain, Pushkin, Tolstoy, and Dostoevsky. I read the poetry of Hugo, Lamartine, and de Musset, and others there is no point in summoning, though I enjoy turning their names over in my memory. After university hunger bit into my belly, and I found nothing to ransom it with but my books. I sold them all to a junk dealer and left the city, to forget. Anyway, here I am now, writing poems in secret and smoking cigarettes in a calm that brings contentment. You haven’t told me, you with the camera: who are you?""",
"notes": [],
},

"12": {
"en": """O captor of shadows, you hold in your hands the riddle of immortality. I was told that whoever the lens of a camera shakes hands with is immortalized forever. Master of the machine, shake my hand with your lens, and I will recite to you a portion of my long exile and a melody from the story of weariness. A man whose years have gone past sixty is not mourned at his passing the way a young man is mourned. No one weeps for someone my age. His right to stay alive has worn away, and he should have made room for someone else aboard this world long ago. How shameless we are! I apologize to you on behalf of all the old men who share the earth’s oxygen with you, and I promise that we will leave soon.

My son, the incisors of exile have gnawed at the loaf of my dreams, and the tongues of strangers have lapped at the vessel of my patience, until I have become what you see: all mockery and sarcasm. Death fears the sarcastic, so the legend of Sindh says. My son, a nation that does not read the faces of its old men the way it reads ancient inscriptions and the ruins of the Thamud is a nation with no sense of history. In the creases of their faces is the story of the prosperity that seeped, with the sweat of their foreheads in the scorching heat, toward you. In their dissolving bones is the secret of the cities that sprouted from barren earth like greens. In the gray of their beards, from long travel, there is a witness and a proof.

I was tossed about the land, east and west, my son, chasing my fleeing luck like a frightened gazelle. Once life offered me an exchange, my luck for a smile that would last a lifetime, and I agreed. I will keep smiling until the last drop of my life, before I leave my place to some new wretch. This is an ember from my exile and a melody from my story. Will your machine immortalize me, O captor of shadows?""",
"notes": ["The Thamud were an ancient Arabian people whose rock-cut tombs and inscriptions survive in northwestern Arabia."],
},

"13": {
"en": """In the presence of bread, swords are disappointed and wither until they dissolve. In the presence of bread, kings sleep in their beds for the first time since Queen Marie Antoinette perished at the hands of hungry bellies, on bread’s command. In the presence of bread, lovers exchange roses, for we “love the rose, but we love bread more… we love the scent of the rose, but the wheat ears are purer than it.” And is our love anything but bread and a kiss?

In the pairing of bread and kiss, man has lived and will live. We squander the purest of our hours hunting bread by day, so that we may enjoy the kiss by night. The love of life, and a person’s wish to stretch upward, make it necessary for him to stretch outward, to seize every resource of living, to expand in every form. And so running after bread each morning is one form of the eternal struggle between man and death. In this picture the loaf is a deadly weapon that dissolves death’s dreams of expansion and of colonizing bodies. Bread is the greatest metaphor humankind has made. Its meaning in the heritage of humanity is vast, so that in some cultures bread has become a synonym for life.

So too the kiss, a delicious form of man’s stretching, and of his buried wish for eternity. If the loaf is a weapon, the plainest sign of man’s struggle with death and his thunderous cry in the face of extinction, then the kiss is a “cold war.” For the kiss, however it veils itself in robes of lofty meaning, such as care, mercy, and concern, can never disguise the meaning of desire, that desire which has immortalized humankind in a chain reaching back into eternity and traveling on forever. I am one of my ancestors who tricked death. How marvelous are bread and the kiss!

I am a baker, and this is my philosophy… Remind me, how many shabati do you want?""",
"notes": ["Shabati is the South Asian flatbread, known elsewhere as chapati."],
},

"14": {
"en": """You asked me, young stranger, about the secret of my gloom, the cause of my low spirits, my reluctant joy, my little warmth toward the sons of Adam, and my celebration of their passing. So take from me a true experience, told in an eloquent voice. By God, I do not know in which direction to pour out what I keep hidden: whether out of my arrogance at being a stranger to you, and your ignorance of me, or out of my need of you, and my wish for an attentive ear that takes from me and gives back to me. I am bound for the goal I have resolved upon, the destination I have turned toward. What drives me to it is the longing to be freed from the pains of the soul, the camel-driver who leads the caravan to the land of mercy. There is no more room in my chest for cigarette butts, and my side no longer has the strength for the bite of the shears.

I will tell you a short story. Understand me, I beg you. It is told in my country that an Indian youth traveled to America for the first time. When the plane landed at the airport of his destination, and every passenger in its belly had got off and left, our friend stayed in the airport for two days. When he was asked why, he answered: “My body arrived quickly, but my soul has not arrived yet, and I am waiting for it.” I used to look on this story with contempt. Who says his soul is not with him?! And who would believe him?! But today I am the one who needs preaching to, more than our Indian friend, and I am worse off. If he waited two days for his soul, I have waited two years for mine, and to this day it has not come. Was my journey faster than it should have been, or was my soul slower than it had to be? I doubt my soul even went with me to the airport.

My soul is hovering now above our house in the village. Sometimes it lands on my little daughter’s brow as a tender kiss that glows with the ache of fatherhood and longing. Sometimes it spends the night in the hair of my loving wife, when winter pitches its tents across the land. It dozes on my mother’s chest when flying has tired it, and it works on the farm with my father, whom neither his bent back nor his softened bones keeps from going out to meet the face of the morning like a duelist.

I love your country as I love my own, but sadly I have lost the fitness for wonder. Here a person accepts everything foreign and strange, unless it is another human being, or so I have felt. You live in a dreadful estrangement while in the very heart of your home. Nothing is left that catches my eye or makes me marvel. I think the chance you gave me, with your kind question about how I am, is the most astonishing thing that has happened to me in a good while, which is why I hurried to pour out my vessel of complaint before you decide to take the chance back. Why are you photographing me while I speak to you? There you are, astonishing me again!""",
"notes": [],
},

"15": {
"en": """Look at my face, my friend, before the borders of identity are rubbed off it, as the borders of my homeland were rubbed off the earth. For the eraser of politics is not content to erase the borders of the land. It goes deeper, to the spirit, the tongue, and the face. Let your eyes wander over the creases of my face and fill your sight with it, then take a step back, or two, and perhaps you will guess who I am.

I am the Bukharan in the dictionary of the Peninsula, the Turkestani in the lexicon of blood, the Uzbek in the eye of the Chinese, and the Chinese only in the eye of the blind. And I am the baker of tamees bread, standing before the riyal.

Look at my face before the fires of the tandoor melt it forever. What is left of it speaks of what was newly won and what was long inherited no more eloquently than the features of my forefathers, men who sold their lives for a cause. Nothing is left of my eyebrows to rise in wonder, nor of my eyelids to shield my eyes from the nightmares of history that appear to me wide awake, in the heart of the day. In the tandoor I spar with, morning and evening, with iron hooks, snatching from it the loaves I sell at the cheapest of prices, I see the image of a Chinese dragon hissing fire. It is the same dragon that drove my people out and blew fire upon their past. I am a Turkestani. I sleep on a dream and wake to a dream. My pillow is a stone of jade, my blanket the dome of Idkah, and my food the stars of Samarkand. “As though Andalusia and Baghdad together had put on, in Turkestan, a crown of glory.”*

*(Yashab, jade: a precious stone for which the city of Kashgar is famous and after which it is named. Idkah: the most famous mosque of Kashgar, built in the ninth century of the Hijra. The verse is by the great poet of Turkestan, the scholar and man of letters Sheikh Abdulaziz Genghis Khan, may God have mercy on him, born in the village of Yeni in 1906 and martyred in Urumqi in 1950 at the hands of the Chinese occupation. He wrote many books, the most important of which is “Turkestan, the Heart of Asia,” considered a reference for the forgotten history of Turkestan. I cannot imagine a Turkestani who loves the land of his ancestors, in whose veins runs the blood of predatory falcons, not making a point of reading this book, for it is written in the ink of resistance, freedom, and love of the land. He also has a collection, “Voice of the Conscience,” in which he gathered his poems and his confessions about his abducted country, Turkestan.)""",
"notes": ["Tamees is a flatbread baked against the wall of a tandoor, a staple of Saudi bakeries and associated with Afghan and Central Asian bakers.", "“Bukharan” was the label commonly given in the Arabian Peninsula to Central Asian migrants of Turkestani origin."],
},
}
